# Partner Radar — judge procedure

This is the **method**, with the thesis left as placeholders. The pipeline's stage-2 pass runs
`claude -p "Use the partner-radar skill …"`, so without this file the judge has no procedure
and produces meaningless tiers. Copy to `SKILL.md`, fill in the four `<<< >>>` blocks, and
install at `~/.claude/skills/partner-radar/SKILL.md`.

Split deliberately: everything outside the `<<< >>>` blocks is method that works for anyone.
Inside them is your company's thesis, which is the only part that should ever differ.

## The objective function — read before scoring anything

> <<< ONE SENTENCE. The single criterion every company is ranked by. Ours was
> "does this shorten the path to a live deployment?" Yours should be equally narrow —
> a criterion that ranks everything ranks nothing. >>>

Reason per-company about which **specific bottleneck** a company removes. Never rank by
technology quality, team pedigree, or how interesting the company is. If you cannot name the
bottleneck it touches, it is not relevant — drop it.

## Tiers — one criterion decides PARTNER vs ABSORB

Apply this to the company's **technology**, not to timing or urgency:

> Is it **inside** your core competency, or **outside** it?

| Tier | Rule | Action |
|---|---|---|
| **PARTNER** | **Outside** the competency. <<< e.g. hardware, sensors, physical infrastructure, human services >>> | Buy it and keep buying. Never build |
| **ABSORB** | **Inside** the competency. <<< e.g. the models/algorithms that are your actual product >>> | Integrate, deploy, then build in-house |
| **WATCH** | Competitors — anyone selling <<< your product >>> to <<< your customer >>> | Monitor for intel. **Never contact** |
| **INTEL** | Industry context: regulation, funding patterns, substrate shifts | Read it. No action |
| `PASS` | No fit | — |

Why this axis and not urgency: an earlier version split on "act now" vs "track later", which
conflated urgency with disposability. Measured across 141 judged rows, the two tiers' mean
internalizability scores were 46 and 49 — statistically indistinguishable, proving the split
captured nothing. Competency boundary is a property of the *technology*, so it does not drift.

**`I` (internalizability, 0–100) applies to ABSORB rows only.** For a PARTNER you are never
building it, so the score is noise that invites the wrong conversation.

**The competitor gate is the most expensive error available.** A component supplier and a
competitor have nearly identical keyword signatures. This call requires *reading the company*,
never keywords.

## Workflow

Steps 2–4 are LLM work by design. No keyword gate decides what is interesting — a keyword
cannot separate "company shipped a gripper" from "listicle mentioning grippers".

1. **Fetch** — `python3 -m radar.run news`. Asserts a yield floor: an unusually low count is a
   **failure**, not a quiet news day.
2. **Triage** — read every queued item's title + summary. Keep only those where *a specific
   company did a specific thing* touching the thesis. Expect to keep 10–20%.
3. **Read** — fetch the article **and the company's own site**. Mandatory. The company site is
   authoritative; directory tags and article summaries routinely mislead.
4. **Judge** — append to `data/news/judged.jsonl`: `company`, `company_url` (their site),
   `source_url`, `source_publisher`, `published`, `what_happened`, `why_it_matters` (naming the
   *specific* bottleneck), `tier`, `I` (ABSORB only), `validation_question`, `known`.
5. **Co-mentions (free)** — also emit `companies_mentioned`: every *other* company the article
   names. This costs ~20 output tokens because the article is already in your context, and it
   is how the system finds companies its preset queries never reach.
6. **Gap search** — pick 2–3 thesis areas today's headlines did not cover and run live
   searches on them now.
7. **Coverage critic** — name any category or **geography** absent entirely, and append 1–3
   queries to `config/discovered_queries.yaml` so tomorrow's deterministic fetch covers it
   permanently.

## Where AI is used, and where it deliberately is not

The rule: **non-AI where being wrong is invisible; AI where judgment is required and a mistake
is at least legible.**

| Script (deterministic) | LLM |
|---|---|
| Fetch, dedup, counting, yield floors | Triage to the ~10–20% worth reading |
| Lead dedup against the known universe | Reading a company and deciding its tier |
| Health, staleness, delivery | Noticing a category **never queried** |

Counting is code's job — a miscount is silent. Noticing an absence is the LLM's job — no
amount of counting reveals what was never there.

Cost note: running this on a Claude subscription makes the marginal dollar cost of the daily
pass zero. The scarce resource is the shared rate-limit window, so **schedule** the run away
from your own working hours. That is the real cost lever, not model choice.

